    "doc_PYafbEOTG9uWOYa8ldFo": {
      "id": "doc_PYafbEOTG9uWOYa8ldFo",
      "workspaceId": "default",
      "canvasId": "cv_n9U5El3G08x7Y1dqFHhk",
      "kind": "block",
      "title": "Untitled",
      "snapshot": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "id": "66d54af4-8d7b-4d81-8928-ac7229baad98",
          "props": {
            "backgroundColor": "default",
            "textColor": "default",
            "textAlignment": "left"
          },
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "An idempotency key is a unique value that a client attaches to a request so that the server can recognise a retry of the same operation.¹ If the server has already completed a request with that key, it returns the stored result instead of performing the operation again.² For example, a checkout client sends ",
              "styles": {}
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Idempotency-Key: 7f3c-91ab",
              "styles": {
                "code": true
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " with a payment request.³ The connection drops before the response arrives, so the client retries with the same key.⁴ The server finds the stored result for ",
              "styles": {}
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "7f3c-91ab",
              "styles": {
                "code": true
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " and returns the original confirmation, and the customer is charged once.⁵ Keys only protect against duplicates while the server still remembers them.⁶ In this design, stored results expire after 24 hours, so a retry sent after that window is treated as a new payment.⁷ Keys also do not make two different requests safe: if a client reuses a key with a different amount, the server must reject the request rather than return the old result.⁸ In short, idempotency keys make retries safe within a bounded window; they are not a general guarantee against duplicate operations.⁹",
              "styles": {}
            }
          ],
          "children": []
        }
      ],
      "createdAt": 1792435918849,
      "updatedAt": 1792435918849,
      "metadata": {
